<% content_for :title, "Stoicism & Bipolar | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="stoicism-bipolar-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="stoicism-bipolar-hero">
    <div class="stoicism-bipolar-hero-content">

      <p class="stoicism-bipolar-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / BIPOLAR / PERSPECTIVES / STOICISM
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>BIPOLAR</span>
        <span>THROUGH A</span>
        <span>STOIC LENS.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="stoicism-bipolar-intro">
        YOU CAN'T CONTROL EVERYTHING.<br>
        YOU CAN CONTROL WHAT YOU DO NEXT.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 -->
  <section class="stoicism-section stoicism-pink">

    <div class="stoicism-section-heading">
      <p class="stoicism-number">
        01 / THE DICHOTOMY OF CONTROL
      </p>

      <h2>
        WHAT'S<br>
        ACTUALLY<br>
        YOURS?
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="stoicism-copy">

      <p class="stoicism-lead">
        Stoicism starts with one brutally useful distinction:
        some things are up to us, and some things aren't.
      </p>

      <p>
        You didn't choose to have bipolar disorder.
        You can't command depression to disappear.
        You can't guarantee that mania will never return.
        You can't control what other people think about your diagnosis.
      </p>

      <p>
        But there are still things you can influence:
        whether you ask for help, whether you follow your treatment plan,
        whether you protect your sleep, whether you use substances,
        and what you do when warning signs appear.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        STOP TRYING TO CONTROL THE UNCONTROLLABLE.
        PUT YOUR ENERGY WHERE IT CAN ACTUALLY DO SOMETHING.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 -->
  <section class="stoicism-section stoicism-yellow">

    <div class="stoicism-section-heading">
      <p class="stoicism-number">
        02 / IMPRESSIONS
      </p>

      <h2>
        FIRST THOUGHT<br>
        ≠ FACT.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="stoicism-copy">

      <p class="stoicism-lead">
        The Stoics paid a lot of attention to the difference
        between what happens and the judgment we immediately
        make about what happens.
      </p>

      <p>
        Your mind produces an impression:
      </p>

      <div class="stoicism-example">

        <p>THE MIND SAYS:</p>

        <h3>
          "EVERYTHING IS FUCKED."
        </h3>

        <p>THE STOIC RESPONSE:</p>

        <h3>
          "THAT IS MY FIRST JUDGMENT. WHAT ARE THE FACTS?"
        </h3>

      </div>

      <p>
        The goal isn't to distrust every thought you have.
        It's to stop automatically treating every thought,
        interpretation, fear, or impulse as established fact.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        AN IMPRESSION CAN KNOCK ON THE DOOR.
        YOU DON'T HAVE TO HAND IT THE KEYS.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 -->
  <section class="stoicism-section stoicism-black">

    <div class="stoicism-section-heading">
      <p class="stoicism-number">
        03 / RESPONSE
      </p>

      <h2>
        PUT SPACE<br>
        BEFORE<br>
        ACTION.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="stoicism-copy">

      <p class="stoicism-lead">
        Bipolar symptoms can create urgency.
        Stoicism gives us a reason to distrust urgency
        just enough to examine it.
      </p>

      <p>
        Anger says respond now.
      </p>

      <p>
        Impulsivity says buy it now.
      </p>

      <p>
        Mania may say start it now.
      </p>

      <p>
        Anxiety says solve it now.
      </p>

      <p>
        But the existence of an urge doesn't require
        immediate action.
      </p>

      <div class="stoicism-pause">

        <span>IMPULSE</span>
        <b>→</b>
        <span>PAUSE</span>
        <b>→</b>
        <span>JUDGMENT</span>
        <b>→</b>
        <span>ACTION</span>

      </div>

      <p>
        Sometimes the most powerful thing you can do is create
        twenty minutes between feeling something and doing something.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 -->
  <section class="stoicism-section stoicism-purple">

    <div class="stoicism-section-heading">
      <p class="stoicism-number">
        04 / ACCEPTANCE
      </p>

      <h2>
        REALITY<br>
        ALREADY<br>
        HAPPENED.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="stoicism-copy">

      <p class="stoicism-lead">
        Acceptance isn't approval.
        It's acknowledging the reality you're actually standing in.
      </p>

      <p>
        Maybe you have bipolar disorder.
        Maybe an episode happened.
        Maybe you fucked something up.
        Maybe somebody left.
        Maybe your life doesn't look the way you thought it would.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can hate that reality.
      </p>

      <p>
        But fighting the fact that it already exists doesn't
        magically replace it with another one.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        THIS IS WHERE I AM.<br>
        NOW WHAT CAN I DO FROM HERE?
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        Stoic acceptance isn't passive.
        It's where useful action begins.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 05 -->
  <section class="stoicism-section stoicism-pink">

    <div class="stoicism-section-heading">
      <p class="stoicism-number">
        05 / CHARACTER
      </p>

      <h2>
        WHO DO<br>
        YOU WANT<br>
        TO BE?
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="stoicism-copy">

      <p class="stoicism-lead">
        Stoicism cares less about whether life feels comfortable
        and more about how you choose to live inside it.
      </p>

      <p>
        The Stoics centered virtues such as wisdom,
        courage, justice, and self-control.
      </p>

      <p>
        Bipolar disorder can affect mood, energy,
        perception, inhibition, and judgment.
      </p>

      <p>
        That makes values useful anchors.
      </p>

      <div class="stoicism-values">

        <div>
          <span>WISDOM</span>
          <p>What is actually true?</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>COURAGE</span>
          <p>What am I avoiding because it's difficult?</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>JUSTICE</span>
          <p>How are my actions affecting other people?</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>SELF-CONTROL</span>
          <p>Do I have to act on this urge?</p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <blockquote>
        YOUR MOOD CAN CHANGE WITHOUT YOUR VALUES
        HAVING TO CHANGE WITH IT.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 06 -->
  <section class="stoicism-section stoicism-yellow">

    <div class="stoicism-section-heading">
      <p class="stoicism-number">
        06 / THE OBSTACLE
      </p>

      <h2>
        USE THE<br>
        FUCKING<br>
        THING.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="stoicism-copy">

      <p class="stoicism-lead">
        Stoicism doesn't require pretending adversity is wonderful.
        It asks what can be done with the reality that exists.
      </p>

      <p>
        Bipolar disorder can take things from you.
        Episodes can cause damage.
        Recovery can require changing parts of your life
        you didn't particularly want to change.
      </p>

      <p>
        None of that has to be romanticized.
      </p>

      <p>
        But hardship can still become information.
      </p>

      <div class="stoicism-lessons">

        <p>
          AN EPISODE CAN TEACH YOU YOUR WARNING SIGNS.
        </p>

        <p>
          A CONSEQUENCE CAN TEACH YOU WHERE A BOUNDARY BELONGS.
        </p>

        <p>
          A RELAPSE CAN SHOW YOU WHERE YOUR PLAN FAILED.
        </p>

        <p>
          A FUCKUP CAN BECOME SOMETHING YOU DON'T REPEAT.
        </p>

      </div>

      <blockquote>
        I DIDN'T ASK FOR THIS.
        BUT I CAN STILL DECIDE WHAT I BUILD FROM IT.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 07 -->
  <section class="stoicism-section stoicism-black">

    <div class="stoicism-section-heading">
      <p class="stoicism-number">
        07 / PUT IT INTO PRACTICE
      </p>

      <h2>
        OKAY.<br>
        NOW WHAT?
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="stoicism-copy">

      <p class="stoicism-lead">
        You don't need a toga, a marble bust, or a copy of
        Marcus Aurelius permanently attached to your hand.
      </p>

      <div class="stoicism-practice-list">

        <div>
          <span>01</span>
          <p>
            <strong>IDENTIFY THE FACTS.</strong><br>
            What actually happened before I add my interpretation?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>02</span>
          <p>
            <strong>CHECK CONTROL.</strong><br>
            Which parts of this are actually mine to influence?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>03</span>
          <p>
            <strong>QUESTION THE IMPRESSION.</strong><br>
            Is my first interpretation necessarily accurate?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>04</span>
          <p>
            <strong>DELAY THE IMPULSE.</strong><br>
            Does anything terrible happen if I don't act right now?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>05</span>
          <p>
            <strong>CHECK YOUR VALUES.</strong><br>
            What would wisdom, courage, fairness, or self-control
            look like here?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>06</span>
          <p>
            <strong>DO WHAT'S YOURS.</strong><br>
            Take the next useful action and release the rest.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ENDING -->
  <section class="stoicism-ending">

    <div>

      <p class="stoicism-ending-small">
        YOU DON'T CONTROL THE WEATHER.
      </p>

      <h2>
        YOU STEER<br>
        <span>THE SHIP.</span>
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="stoicism-ending-copy">

      <p>
        You don't control every thought.
      </p>

      <p>
        You don't control every emotion.
      </p>

      <p>
        You don't control every circumstance.
      </p>

      <p>
        And bipolar disorder means there may be times when
        your ability to choose and judge clearly is genuinely impaired.
      </p>

      <p>
        The point isn't perfect control.
        It's practicing agency wherever agency is available:
        noticing, questioning, preparing, asking for help,
        and choosing the next useful action.
      </p>

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO PERSPECTIVES",
          bipolar_perspectives_path,
          class: "stoicism-back-button" %>

    </div>

  </section>

</main>